*leans back in chair, removes glasses, cleans them thoughtfully*

Let me tell you something. When I was at Notre Dame, I learned that the most elegant solutions are often the simplest ones. A shopping cart - it's like governing. You need to know what's in it, what it costs, and when to empty it.

What's next? Implementation.

```go
package cart

import "errors"

var ErrItemNotFound = errors.New("item not found")

type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(item Item) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[item.ID]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += item.Quantity
	} else {
		newItem := item
		c.items[item.ID] = &newItem
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) error {
	if _, ok := c.items[id]; !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	delete(c.items, id)
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(id string, quantity int) error {
	item, ok := c.items[id]
	if !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	if quantity <= 0 {
		delete(c.items, id)
	} else {
		item.Quantity = quantity
	}
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) (*Item, error) {
	item, ok := c.items[id]
	if !ok {
		return nil, ErrItemNotFound
	}
	return item, nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	result := make([]Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, *item)
	}
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}
```

*stands, walks to window*

You see, I was raised by a man who believed you don't add complexity where none is required. The tests asked for a cart that adds items, removes them, updates quantities, calculates totals. Not one for authentication, not one for persistence, not one for discount codes.

So I gave them exactly what they asked for. A map for O(1) lookups. Pointer storage to allow in-place updates. Defensive copies when returning items. Error handling precisely where the tests expect it.

*turns back*

The ItemCount returns total quantity of all items - not unique items. Why? Because when someone asks "how many things are in my cart," they mean "I have 3 apples and 2 oranges, that's 5 things." The tests will confirm this.

UpdateQuantity to zero or below removes the item entirely. Clean. Idiomatic. What the tests require.

